insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, description, body, nav_label, show_in_nav, sort_order, seo_title, seo_description, noindex)
values ('player-code-of-conduct', 'U10 Player Code of Conduct', 'Clear, age-appropriate team rules for U10 players at Fusion FC.', $md$At Fusion FC, we want football to be fun, safe, and respectful for everyone. These are our team rules for U10 players.

## We show respect
- We listen when coaches or referees speak.
- We are kind to teammates, opponents, and spectators.
- We use good language and good manners.

## We work hard
- We arrive on time and ready to train.
- We try our best, even when things feel difficult.
- We keep going and learn from mistakes.

## We play fair
- We follow the rules of the game.
- We do not cheat, dive, or argue.
- We shake hands and say "well played" after matches.

## We are good teammates
- We pass, communicate, and include everyone.
- We clap effort, not just goals.
- We help each other improve.

## We stay safe and responsible
- We wear the right kit and shin pads.
- We bring water and look after our equipment.
- We tell a coach if we are hurt or worried.

## If we get it wrong
Everyone makes mistakes. We will:
1. Get a reminder.
2. Talk with the coach.
3. Involve parents if needed.

Then we reset and try again.
$md$, 'Player Conduct', true, 4, 'Fusion FC U10 Player Code of Conduct', 'Simple and clear behaviour standards for U10 players at Fusion FC.', false)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

insert into public.posts (slug, title, date, excerpt, body, cover_image, tags, author, draft, seo_title, seo_description)
values ('ali-johnson-head-of-girls-football', 'Ali Johnson Appointed Head of Girls Football', '2026-03-20', 'Fusion FC is delighted to announce Ali Johnson as our new Head of Girls Football, leading the launch of our girls teams for the 2026/27 season.', $md$We're excited to announce that **Ali Johnson** has been appointed as our **Head of Girls Football**.

Ali will oversee the launch and development of our girls section, marking an exciting new chapter for the club.

Following the success of our [girls development sessions](/development-sessions) on Wednesday evenings, we're now preparing to launch our girls teams ahead of the **2026/27 season**.

Ali has played a huge role in getting us to this point, and it's fantastic to have someone so passionate and dedicated leading the way as we continue to grow the girls game at our club.

## Building on Strong Foundations

Our [Wednesday evening development sessions](/development-sessions) have been running successfully, providing a welcoming and supportive environment for girls of all abilities to develop their skills and love for the game.

With Ali now leading the programme, we're excited to build on this foundation and establish competitive teams that will represent Fusion FC in the 2026/27 season.

## Get Involved

If you're interested in joining our girls football programme, or would like more information about upcoming sessions, please get in touch through our [contact page](/contact) or speak to Ali at our [Wednesday evening development sessions](/development-sessions).

We're looking for players of all abilities who want to be part of something special as we launch this exciting new chapter for Fusion FC.

Watch this space for more updates on trials, training schedules, and team announcements!
$md$, '/images/ali-johnson-head-of-girls-football.jpg', array['Club News', 'Girls Football', 'Coaching']::text[], 'Fusion Football Club', false, 'Ali Johnson Appointed Head of Girls Football at Fusion FC', 'Ali Johnson has been appointed Head of Girls Football at Fusion FC, leading the development and launch of our girls teams for the 2026/27 season.')
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

insert into public.posts (slug, title, date, excerpt, body, cover_image, tags, author, draft, seo_title, seo_description)
values ('her-game-too-partnership', 'Fusion FC × Her Game Too: A Partnership Built on Inclusion', '2026-05-20', 'Fusion FC is proud to announce a partnership with Her Game Too, the UK-wide campaign tackling sexism in football and making the game more welcoming for women and girls at every level.', $md$<p align="center">
  <img src="/brand/partners/her-game-too.png" alt="Her Game Too logo" width="220" />
</p>

Fusion FC is proud to announce that we have officially partnered with **[Her Game Too](https://hergametoo.co.uk/)**, a UK-wide campaign committed to tackling sexism in football and making the game a more welcoming place for women and girls at every level.

It's a partnership that reflects who we are as a club, and where we want the game to go.

## About Her Game Too

Her Game Too was founded in 2021 by Caz May and Lucy Ford in response to the sexist abuse and discrimination female football fans regularly face in grounds, online, and throughout the game. What started as a small campaign has grown into a national movement, with clubs from the Premier League down to grassroots level signing up to publicly back the cause.

Their mission is straightforward: **football is for everyone**.

## Why This Matters to Fusion

At Fusion FC, inclusion is not a slogan. It's how we run the club. We have a growing girls' section and a commitment to creating an environment where every player, parent, and supporter feels they belong.

Partnering with Her Game Too gives us a framework to formalise that commitment. It puts our name alongside hundreds of other clubs across the country who have publicly said: *this matters, and we're going to do something about it.*

## A Word from Ali Johnson

Our **Head of Girls Football, Ali Johnson**, has been driving the growth of the girls' game at Fusion and was central to bringing this partnership over the line.

> "Becoming a Her Game Too partner club is a really proud moment for us. Every girl who walks into one of our sessions deserves to feel like she belongs on that pitch. No questions, no caveats. This partnership says exactly that, and it holds us to it.
>
> I got into coaching the girls' game because of my daughter. Watching her fall in love with football made me realise how important it is that the environment around her matches the talent and enthusiasm she brings to it. Every girl deserves that, and as a club we have a responsibility to make sure it happens.
>
> I want other parents to know: if you've ever felt unsure about whether grassroots football is a welcoming place for your daughter, we want to show you that it can be. Come down to a session. See for yourself."

## What This Looks Like in Practice

Being a Her Game Too partner club is not a badge to stick on a website and forget about. It comes with real commitments:

- **Visible support:** displaying Her Game Too branding across our channels and at sessions, making it clear where the club stands.
- **Education and awareness:** sharing the campaign's resources with players, coaches, and parents so everyone understands the *why*, not just the *what*.
- **A better environment:** continuing to build a club culture where every girl and woman, whether player, coach, or supporter, is respected and valued.
- **Growing the girls' game:** working alongside Ali and our coaching team to create more opportunities for female players across every age group.

## Be Part of It

If you'd like to learn more about the partnership, our values, and what it means for the club, head to our **[Her Game Too partnership page](/her-game-too/)**.

And if you have a daughter who's curious about football, our [Wednesday evening development sessions](/development-sessions/) are the best place to start. Ali and the team would love to meet you.

A huge thank you to **[Her Game Too](https://hergametoo.co.uk/)** for the work they do, and for welcoming Fusion FC into the movement. We're proud to be part of it.
$md$, '/brand/partners/her-game-too.png', array['Club News', 'Girls Football', 'Community', 'Inclusion']::text[], 'Fusion Football Club', false, 'Fusion FC Partners With Her Game Too', 'Fusion FC is proud to partner with Her Game Too, the UK campaign tackling sexism in football. Ali Johnson, Head of Girls Football, shares what the partnership means for the club.')
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

insert into public.posts (slug, title, date, excerpt, body, cover_image, tags, author, draft, seo_title, seo_description)
values ('sponsorship-2026-27-season', 'Partner With Us: Sponsorship Opportunities for the 2026/27 Season', '2026-03-08', 'Fusion FC is actively looking for local businesses to partner with for the upcoming season. With sixteen teams, development sessions, and a growing online presence, there has never been a better time to get involved.', $md$Grassroots football runs on community support. The pitches, the kit, the coaching, the matchday experience: none of it happens without the people and businesses who believe in what clubs like ours are trying to build.

As we head into the 2026/27 season, Fusion FC is actively looking for local businesses to come on board as partners. Whether you are a sole trader, a small business, or an established company, there is a sponsorship package that works for you, and a genuine opportunity to make a meaningful difference to young players in your area.

## Sixteen Teams. Hundreds of Touchpoints.

We are fielding sixteen teams this season across two matchday programmes:

**Saturday: Staffordshire Junior Football League**
- U10 Playing Up, coached by Russ Leadbetter
- U10 Black Playing Up, coached by Ali Johnson
- U10, coached by Mark Woolridge
- U9 White, coached by Sam Rosillo
- U8, coached by Ryan Foster
- U8 White, coached by James Wilson

**Sunday: NSJYL (North Staffs Junior Youth League)**
- U11, coached by Tom Pope
- U10 Green, coached by Sam Cummins
- U10 Black, coached by Mark Woolridge
- U10 Green Playing Up, coached by Ben Chapman
- U10 White, coached by Jake Hodgkinson
- U9, coached by Lee Ralphson
- U9 Green, coached by Greg Woodcock
- U9 Black, coached by Jon Bloor
- U8 Green, coached by Ryan Foster
- U8 Black, coached by Tom Kilgariff

Each team plays regularly across the season, trains weekly, and is supported by coaches invested in each player's development. That is a lot of touchpoints: matchdays, training sessions, social posts, kit days, and end-of-season events, where your brand can be part of the story.

## What Sponsors Receive

We want partnerships to work for the businesses that make them possible. Depending on the level you choose, sponsorship with Fusion FC can include:

**Brand Visibility**
Your business name and logo featured on the club website, including a dedicated sponsor profile page and a link back to your site. The site is growing steadily with families, coaches, and local supporters visiting regularly throughout the season.

**Social Media Exposure**
Shoutouts across our social channels when deals are agreed, at key points in the season, and whenever your support directly helps the club. We talk about our sponsors because we are genuinely grateful; it is not just a logo placement.

**Kit and Matchday Presence**
Depending on the package, your business name can appear on training kit or matchday shirts, putting your brand in front of parents, spectators, referees, and opposition players at every fixture.

**Community Association**
This is one that does not show up in a spreadsheet but matters enormously. Being known as a business that supports local youth football carries real weight in the community. Parents talk. Schools talk. Local networks are tight, and being a good community contributor builds trust that advertising cannot buy.

## Why Sponsor a Grassroots Club?

The honest answer: because it matters.

Professional football gets the headlines, but grassroots is where the game actually lives. It is where a child kicks a ball for the first time and decides they love it. It is where young players learn to lose gracefully and win with humility. It is where families spend their Saturday and Sunday mornings, standing on touchlines, cheering each other on.

When you sponsor Fusion FC, your money does not disappear into a marketing budget. It buys kit that keeps a child warm in January. It covers pitch hire so a team can train. It funds equipment that makes sessions better. Every pound is visible and every pound counts.

And in return, you get your brand associated with all of that: community, with youth, with doing something genuinely good.

## Who We Are Looking For

We are open to all kinds of partnerships. If you are a local business of any size and you care about the area you operate in, we want to talk to you. Previous sponsors have included trades, professional services, retail, and hospitality. The common thread is local roots and a willingness to back something worth backing.

There is no minimum spend. We will work with you to find a level that makes sense, with clear visibility on what your support will deliver.

## Get in Touch

If you are interested in becoming a Fusion FC sponsor for the 2026/27 season, the best first step is to reach out. You can:

- **Visit our sponsor page** for more information on how partnerships work
- **Use the contact form** to get in touch directly with the club
- **Email us** at info@fusionfc.co.uk

We will get back to you quickly, answer any questions, and talk through what a partnership could look like for your business.

---

*Fusion FC is a grassroots football club supporting players aged 8 to 11 across sixteen registered teams competing in the Staffordshire Junior Football League on Saturdays and the North Staffs Junior Youth League on Sundays.*
$md$, '/brand/blog/sponsorship-2026-27.svg', array['Sponsors', 'Community', 'Club News']::text[], 'Fusion Football Club', false, 'Sponsor Fusion FC: Partnership Opportunities 2026/27', 'Fusion FC is looking for local business sponsors for the 2026/27 season. Get your brand in front of families and the local community through our teams, website, and socials.')
on conflict (slug) do nothing;
